"To determine the order of rotation barriers about the C=C bond, we need to consider the substituents attached to the double-bonded carbons. A higher steric and electronic interaction generally increases the rotation barrier.\n\n1. **Structure I (CH\u2082=CH\u2082):**\n   - Ethene (H\u2082C=CH\u2082) has the smallest steric hindrance and no electron-withdrawing or donating groups. \n   - Therefore, it has the lowest rotation barrier.\n\n2. **Structure II (CH\u2083O-CH=CH\u2082):**\n   - The presence of a methoxy group (CH\u2083O-) introduces resonance and slight steric hindrance.\n   - Increased electron donation from the methoxy group slightly raises the barrier compared to ethene.\n\n3. **Structure III (CH\u2083O-CH=CH-COOEt):**\n   - The methoxy group is electron-donating, and the ester group (COOEt) is electron-withdrawing.\n   - The combination of resonance and electronic effects increases the barrier significantly.\n\nIn conclusion:\n- Structure III has the highest rotation barrier due to the electron-withdrawing and resonance effects.\n- Structure II has a moderate barrier because of the methoxy group.\n- Structure I has the lowest barrier.\n\nThus, the correct order of rotation barriers is III > II > I.\n\nANSWER: B. III > II > I."
