"To calculate the opportunity cost of producing Glasses in Mexico, we need to understand how many units of Shoes Mexico has to forego in order to produce one unit of Glasses.\n\nIn Mexico:\n- It takes 10 workers to produce 1 unit of Shoes.\n- It takes 12 workers to produce 1 unit of Glasses.\n\nThe opportunity cost of producing one unit of Glasses is the amount of Shoes that could have been produced with those same 12 workers.\n\nNumber of Shoes that can be produced with 12 workers = \\( \\frac{12 \\text{ workers}}{10 \\text{ workers per Shoe}} = 1.2 \\) Shoes.\n\nTherefore, the opportunity cost of producing one unit of Glasses in Mexico is 1.2 Shoes.\n\nANSWER: A. 1.20 Shoes."
"To determine which country should specialize in the production of Shoes, we need to compare the opportunity cost for each country.\n\n**Opportunity Cost Calculation:**\n\n1. **Mexico:**\n   - 10 workers for 1 unit of Shoes.\n   - 12 workers for 1 unit of Glasses.\n\n   Opportunity cost of Shoes in terms of Glasses: \\(10/12 = 0.833\\) Glasses per Shoe.\n\n2. **Nigeria:**\n   - 18 workers for 1 unit of Shoes.\n   - 5 workers for 1 unit of Glasses.\n\n   Opportunity cost of Shoes in terms of Glasses: \\(18/5 = 3.6\\) Glasses per Shoe.\n\nSince Mexico has the lower opportunity cost for producing Shoes (0.833 Glasses per Shoe compared to Nigeria's 3.6 Glasses per Shoe), Mexico should specialize in the production of Shoes.\n\nANSWER: C."
