"To find the value of \\( C_s \\) for full compensation for the inverter circuit, we will derive the Miller capacitance for the transistor.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Transistor Parameters:**\n   - \\( h_{FE(min)} = 60 \\)\n   - \\( r_{b'e} = 1200 \\, \\Omega \\)\n   - \\( C_i = 100 \\, \\text{pF} \\)\n   - \\( V_{BE(ON)} = 0.7 \\, \\text{V} \\)\n   - \\( V_{sat} = 0.2 \\, \\text{V} \\)\n   - \\( V_i = 6 \\, \\text{V} \\)\n\n2. **Calculate Base Current (\\( I_B \\)):**\n   - Assume the base-emitter junction is ON when \\( V_i = 6 \\, \\text{V} \\).\n   - Voltage across the base-emitter junction: \\( V_{BE} = V_i - V_{BE(ON)} = 6V - 0.7V = 5.3V \\).\n   - Base current into \\( r_{b'e} \\): \\( I_B = \\frac{5.3 \\, \\text{V}}{1200 \\, \\Omega} \\).\n\n3. **Calculate Collector Current (\\( I_C \\)):**\n   - Using \\( I_C = h_{FE(min)} \\times I_B \\).\n\n4. **Use Miller Effect for Capacitance:**\n   - Input capacitance due to Miller: \\( C_{Miller} = C_i \\times (1 + h_{FE(min)}) = 100 \\, \\text{pF} \\times (1 + 60) \\).\n\n5. **required Base-Emitter Capacitance for Full Compensation:**\n   - \\( C_{total} = C_i + C_{Miller} \\)\n\n   Assuming the Miller capacitance mainly drives the required compensating effect, hence the compensating capacitance \\( C_s \\) should be:\n\n   \\[\n   C_s = C_M - C_i = C_i \\times (h_{FE(min)}) = 100 \\, \\text{pF} \\times 60 \n   \\]\n\n6. **Solution:**\n   - Calculate \\( C_s = 6000 \\, \\text{pF} \\).\n\nCheck the options given (A, B, C, D, etc.) against calculated value, and choose the closest option.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To determine the minimum \\( R_L \\) so that the output voltage is in the range of logical 1 (4V to 5V) when the input voltage \\( v_1 \\) is zero, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify Key Voltages:**\n   - \\( V_{CC} = 5V \\)\n   - Logical 1 output range is 4V to 5V.\n\n2. **Assumptions:**\n   - When \\( v_1 = 0 \\), the transistor is off.\n   - In this state, there is no current through \\( R_C \\), hence no voltage drop across it.\n\n3. **Calculate \\( R_L \\) to Maintain Output Voltage:**\n   - For \\( v_1 = 0 \\), the entire \\( V_{CC} \\) appears across \\( R_L \\).\n   - Use Ohm's Law: \\( V = I \\cdot R \\)\n   - Rearrange it to find \\( R_L \\):\n     \\[\n     R_L = \\frac{V_{out}}{I_L}\n     \\]\n   - Since minimum \\( V_{out} = 4V \\), maximum \\( I_L = \\frac{V_{CC} - V_{out}}{R_C} \\).\n\n4. **Substitute the Given Values:**\n   \\[\n   I_L = \\frac{5V - 4V}{5k\\Omega} = \\frac{1V}{5k\\Omega} = 0.2mA\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate \\( R_L \\):**\n   \\[\n   R_L = \\frac{4V}{0.2 \\times 10^{-3}A} = 20k\\Omega\n   \\]\n\nThus, the minimum \\( R_L \\) to ensure an output voltage of at least 4V is 20k\\Omega.\n\nANSWER: A"
