"To determine which historical process is reflected by the movements shown in the table, let's analyze the data:\n\n1. The table shows population movements between India, West Pakistan, and East Pakistan from 1947 to 1951.\n2. The migrations are categorized into two main groups: \n   - From India to West and East Pakistan, primarily Muslim populations (marked with an asterisk).\n   - From West and East Pakistan to India, primarily Hindu or Sikh populations (marked with two asterisks).\n3. The figures represent large-scale population exchanges, specifically due to religious affiliations.\n  \nThis pattern relates closely to the events that occurred during the partition of India in 1947, when British India was divided into the two independent dominions of India and Pakistan. This division led to massive population exchanges based on religious lines, with Muslims moving to Pakistan (West and East) and Hindus and Sikhs moving to India.\n\nLet's evaluate the options:\n\nA. The migration of former colonial subjects to cities in the former colonizing country.  \n   - This does not fit the context as it refers to migration to a colonial power, whereas the data shows movement between newly independent states.\n\nB. The development of ethnic enclaves as migrants moved to seek work opportunities.  \n   - While enclaves may have formed, this does not fully capture the large-scale exchange prompted by the partition.\n\nC. The migration patterns associated with male seasonal laborers.  \n   - The data indicates widespread and permanent migrations, not seasonal labor movements.\n\nD. The population resettlement caused by redrawing former colonial boundaries.  \n   - This accurately describes the historical process of partition, where the boundary drawing between India and Pakistan caused significant population resettlement based on religion.\n\nANSWER: D."
