"To determine the type of selectivity observed in Step 1, let's analyze the reaction:\n\n1. **Reaction Details**: \n   - Friedel-Crafts acylation with the starting compound being a dichlorobenzene derivative and succinic anhydride in the presence of AlCl\u2083.\n   - The reaction introduces an acyl group onto the aromatic ring.\n\n2. **Types of Selectivity**:\n   - **Chemoselectivity**: Preference for one functional group over another. Not applicable here as only one functional group reacts.\n   - **Regioselectivity**: Preference for one direction of chemical bond making/breaking over all other possible directions. This is applicable since the acyl group prefers one position on the aromatic ring.\n   - **Stereospecificity**: Specific stereoisomers are formed. Not relevant here as no stereocenters are involved in this step.\n   - **Enantioselectivity**: Formation of one enantiomer over another. Not relevant here as no chiral centers are formed in this step.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Friedel-Crafts acylation on a dichlorobenzene favors a specific position relative to the substituents already on the benzene ring due to electronic and steric effects. Thus, the reaction is regioselective, introducing the acyl group at a preferred position.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To determine the saponification products of the given compound, we need to recognize it as a triglyceride. Triglycerides are composed of glycerol and three fatty acid chains linked by ester bonds.\n\nWhen triglycerides undergo saponification, they react with sodium hydroxide (NaOH) to produce glycerol and the sodium salts of the fatty acids. The ester bonds are broken in this hydrolysis reaction.\n\nUpon hydrolysis:\n\n1. The glycerol backbone is released.\n2. Each fatty acid is converted into its sodium salt.\n\nTherefore, the saponification products will be both glycerol and the sodium salts of the presented fatty acids.\n\nANSWER: C. Both of them."
